/// Inline `<T: Bound>` constraints - the syntax nothing else in the tree writes.
///
/// WHAT THIS PINS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
///
/// It pins that the syntax parses, compiles and runs. It does NOT pin bound
/// enforcement, because there is none to pin: measured two ways on this tree,
///
///   * dropping the constraint (`<T: Tagged>` -> `<T>`) compiles just the same,
///     so the bound is not load-bearing for the body's `x.tag()`;
///   * instantiating with a type that does NOT implement the trait is rejected
///     as `E0358: no method named 'tag'` - a method-resolution failure in the
///     body, the identical error an unbounded `<T>` produces. Nothing reports a
///     violated bound.
///
/// So an inline constraint is currently parsed, stored, resolved to a trait
/// TypeRef, and then observed by nothing. Do not read a pass here as evidence
/// that a bound was checked. If bound enforcement is ever implemented, the
/// negative case belongs beside this file.
///
/// WHY IT EXISTS ANYWAY
///
/// A `where` bound and an inline constraint travel through DIFFERENT AST
/// fields: the first becomes a `TraitBound`, the second becomes
/// `GenericParamNode.constraints`. Only the second reaches the constraint loop
/// in `create_generic_param_types`, and the tree writes `where` everywhere -
/// 398 where-clauses across 86 stdlib files and not one inline constraint
/// outside a docstring, in stdlib, compiler/src, examples or tests.
///
/// That loop was therefore unreachable by every corpus in the repository, so a
/// change to it - or a deletion of it - looked inert on all of them. This file
/// is what makes it reachable. A counter over that code reads the same zero
/// whether it is starved or dead; this project is the difference.
namespace InlineGenericBound::Main;

type trait Tagged {
    tag(&this) -> i32;
}

type struct Widget {
    v: i32;
}

implement trait Tagged for Widget {
    tag(&this) -> i32 { return this.v; }
}

/// Reaches the constraint loop via a free function's own generic params.
function tag_of<T: Tagged>(x: &T) -> i32 {
    return x.tag();
}

/// And via a declaration's, which is routed to the loop by a different caller.
type struct Holder<T: Tagged> {
    item: T;
}

function main() -> i32 {
    mut w: Widget = Widget { v: 7 };
    const a: i32 = tag_of<Widget>(&w);
    mut h: Holder<Widget> = Holder<Widget> { item: Widget { v: 5 } };
    return a - h.item.tag() - 2;
}
